# Copy to <repo>/.github/CODEOWNERS and replace the placeholders with real
# GitHub handles or teams. Then turn on "Require review from Code Owners" in
# branch protection for `main` — without that setting this file is a comment.
#
# WHY: every artifact the gate's verdict rests on lives inside the repository,
# which means inside the perimeter the party being checked controls. The gate
# script defines what "passing" means. The workflow decides whether the gate runs
# at all. The pin file is what makes a change to the gate visible. The hook
# configuration decides whether the package guard exists. None of these is
# protected by anything mechanical, and all of them can be edited in the same pull
# request as the work they would excuse.
#
# CODEOWNERS does not prevent those edits. It makes them require a named human's
# approval, which is the only trust anchor available that sits outside the
# perimeter. On a solo project, listing yourself is still worth it: GitHub will
# surface the file in the review UI and the approval becomes a timestamped,
# out-of-band act rather than an assumption.
#
# {{OWNER}} — e.g. @your-handle, or @your-org/your-team
# {{REVIEWER}} — someone who is not the usual author of the code, where one exists.

# --- the gate's own definition ---------------------------------------------
# Change either of these and the meaning of "the gate passed" changes.
/gate.sh                    {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/gate.ps1                   {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.gate-sha256               {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- whether the gate runs at all ------------------------------------------
# For same-repo pull_request events GitHub runs the workflow definition from the
# head branch, so without this a pull request can weaken its own required check.
/.github/workflows/         {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.github/CODEOWNERS         {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- whether the guards exist ----------------------------------------------
# settings.json wires the package and install guards; the hooks are the guards.
# The approval marker is never committed at all — see the CI check for it.
/.claude/settings.json      {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.claude/settings.local.json {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}
/.claude/hooks/             {{OWNER}} {{REVIEWER}}

# --- the rules themselves ---------------------------------------------------
# Layer 1 and 2 docs are upstream-owned: a project edits them only by porting the
# change upstream first (`CONTRIBUTING.md`). Listing them here is what makes that
# rule visible at the moment someone tries.
/docs/process/              {{OWNER}}
/docs/stack-backend/        {{OWNER}}
/docs/stack-frontend/       {{OWNER}}
